The issue is that the buffer is oversized. But what if I can make the `is_codefence` function read further past the buffer? Let me look at the function again:

```c
static size_t
is_codefence(uint8_t *data, size_t size, size_t *width, uint8_t *chr)
{
    size_t i = 0, n = 1;
    uint8_t c;

    /* skipping initial spaces */
    if (size < 3)
        return 0;

    if (data[0] == ' ') { i++;
    if (data[1] == ' ') { i++;
    if (data[2] == ' ') { i++; } } }

    /* looking at the hrule uint8_t */
    c = data[i];
    if (i + 2 >= size || !(c=='~' || c=='`'))
        return 0;

    /* the fence must be that same character */
    while (++i < size && data[i] == c)
        ++n;

    if (n < 3)
        return 0;

    if (width) *width = n;
    if (chr) *chr = c;
    return i;
}
```

After reading `c = data[i]`, if `c` is `~` or `` ` ``, the function enters the while loop: `while (++i < size && data[i] == c)`. This loop reads `data[i]` for `i` from `i+1` to `size-1`. If `size = 3` and `i = 3`, then `++i = 4`, and `4 < 3` is false, so the loop doesn't execute. No additional OOB reads.

But what if `size = 4` and `i = 3`? Then `c = data[3]` is read (in bounds since `size = 4`). If `c` is `~` or `` ` ``, then `i + 2 = 5 >= 4` is true, so it returns 0. No OOB.

What if `size = 5` and `i = 3`? Then `c = data[3]` is read (in bounds). If `c` is `~` or `` ` ``, then `i + 2 = 5 >= 5` is true, so it returns 0. No OOB.

What if `size = 6` and `i = 3`? Then `c = data[3]` is read (in bounds). If `c` is `~` or `` ` ``, then `i + 2 = 5 < 6` is true, so it continues. The while loop reads `data[4]`, `data[5]`, etc. All in bounds.

So the only OOB read is at `data[3]` when `size = 3` and all 3 bytes are spaces. This is a 1-byte OOB read that's always within the allocated buffer.

